Northern Lights by Adil Kairolla (MacPaper Engine)
https://github.com/adilkairolla/macpaper-engine/tree/main/Samples/Northern%20Lights.macpaper

This is the "Northern Lights" sample wallpaper of the MacPaper Engine project
(https://github.com/adilkairolla/macpaper-engine), ported to Metal Shading Language
for MacOS Live Wallpaper. The sample is procedural: it ships no images, and its
shaders were added to the repository by its author in the project's first commit.

Licence statement relied on. The repository's LICENSE file
(https://github.com/adilkairolla/macpaper-engine/blob/main/LICENSE, as of commit
5e316a7edcf79e6472920718c732b8d8bcfa541f) reads, in its opening lines:

    MIT License

    Copyright (c) 2026 Adil Kairolla

    Permission is hereby granted, free of charge, to any person obtaining a copy
    of this software and associated documentation files (the "Software"), to deal
    in the Software without restriction, ...

and the project's README.md, in its "License" section, says: "[MIT](LICENSE) © Adil
Kairolla". The shader files of the sample carry no notice of their own, so this
repository-wide MIT licence is the statement that covers them.

Note on provenance. The helper functions mp_hash, mp_noise and mp_fbm in main.metal
are copied from the same repository (Sources/MacPaper/Render/ShaderSource.swift),
where they carry no separate attribution. mp_hash is a three-line hash of a kind
that is widely published in shader tutorials and code; no other author's shader is
credited or reproduced in the sample.

What was changed (the full list is at the top of main.metal): the five layers
(sky, stars, aurora, mountains, fog) are drawn in one pass; the "stars" particle
emitter is drawn per pixel from a jittered grid with the same size, lifetime, fade
and twinkle settings, the twinkle at half speed; the sample's default parameter
values are constants; the camera sway ("drift") is kept and computed from time; the
canvas fills the screen's height and continues sideways on wide screens; the ridge
edges are anti-aliased over one pixel; and the code is written as a mainImage
function for this app. The picture is otherwise the sample's.

